1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,459
A letter travels across India
and lands at one single house.

2
00:00:04,859 --> 00:00:07,611
Country, state, city,
street, house number.

3
00:00:07,611 --> 00:00:10,163
Each line of the address
narrows the search.

4
00:00:10,613 --> 00:00:14,677
Aarav was writing an address and
asked: does every living thing have an

5
00:00:14,677 --> 00:00:15,884
address like this?

6
00:00:16,234 --> 00:00:17,134
It does.

7
00:00:16,959 --> 00:00:20,891
And it works the same way, from
huge groups down to one kind.

8
00:00:21,391 --> 00:00:25,682
By the end, you will know the seven
taxonomic categories, and the one rule

9
00:00:25,682 --> 00:00:27,151
that runs through them.

10
00:00:27,801 --> 00:00:30,491
Classification is not one single step.

11
00:00:30,841 --> 00:00:32,538
It is a ladder of steps.

12
00:00:32,538 --> 00:00:35,670
Each step is a rank, called
a taxonomic category.

13
00:00:36,120 --> 00:00:40,857
All the categories together, stacked
in order, form the taxonomic hierarchy.

14
00:00:41,307 --> 00:00:44,501
And any real group at any
rank is called a taxon.

15
00:00:44,501 --> 00:00:46,850
More than one taxon are called taxa.

16
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:51,008
Let's walk wheat down its
address, one category at a time.

17
00:00:51,358 --> 00:00:52,258
Kingdom.

18
00:00:52,033 --> 00:00:55,207
Wheat sits in Plantae,
with every plant on Earth.

19
00:00:55,707 --> 00:00:56,607
Division.

20
00:00:56,482 --> 00:00:59,255
For plants we say division, not phylum.

21
00:00:59,255 --> 00:01:02,435
Wheat is in Angiospermae,
the flowering plants.

22
00:01:02,935 --> 00:01:03,835
Class.

23
00:01:03,583 --> 00:01:07,542
Monocotyledonae: plants whose
seed has a single cotyledon.

24
00:01:08,042 --> 00:01:09,111
Order, Poales.

25
00:01:09,111 --> 00:01:11,067
Family, Poaceae, the grasses.

26
00:01:11,567 --> 00:01:12,942
Genus, Triticum.

27
00:01:12,942 --> 00:01:14,707
And species, aestivum.

28
00:01:14,707 --> 00:01:18,021
Together: Triticum
aestivum, one single kind.

29
00:01:18,671 --> 00:01:21,640
Now watch what changes
as we climb back up.

30
00:01:21,990 --> 00:01:25,482
At the species level, members
share almost every feature.

31
00:01:25,482 --> 00:01:27,163
They look and live alike.

32
00:01:27,613 --> 00:01:30,240
Each step up adds more kinds to the group.

33
00:01:30,240 --> 00:01:32,103
But they share fewer features.

34
00:01:32,553 --> 00:01:37,170
By the kingdom level, the group is
huge, and members share only a few basic

35
00:01:37,170 --> 00:01:38,070
features.

36
00:01:38,536 --> 00:01:41,133
So here is the rule, one step at a time.

37
00:01:41,483 --> 00:01:42,383
Step one.

38
00:01:42,331 --> 00:01:46,469
Species is the lowest category,
for plants and animals alike.

39
00:01:47,219 --> 00:01:48,119
Step two.

40
00:01:48,003 --> 00:01:52,810
Going up from species to kingdom, the
number of shared features keeps falling.

41
00:01:53,560 --> 00:01:54,460
Step three.

42
00:01:54,418 --> 00:01:57,964
The lower the taxon, the more
features its members share.

43
00:01:58,714 --> 00:01:59,614
Step four.

44
00:01:59,531 --> 00:02:03,748
The higher the category, the harder
it is to judge how its members are

45
00:02:03,748 --> 00:02:04,648
related.

46
00:02:05,165 --> 00:02:08,567
Now, one word that confuses
many students: taxon.

47
00:02:08,917 --> 00:02:10,440
Insects are a taxon.

48
00:02:10,440 --> 00:02:13,538
All insects share three
pairs of jointed legs.

49
00:02:13,988 --> 00:02:18,236
Animals, mammals and dogs are all
taxa too, at different levels.

50
00:02:18,236 --> 00:02:21,233
A dog is a mammal, and
mammals are animals.

51
00:02:21,833 --> 00:02:22,733
Your turn.

52
00:02:22,720 --> 00:02:27,500
Who share more features: two members
of one genus, or two members of one

53
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:28,400
class?

54
00:02:28,121 --> 00:02:29,426
Pause and decide.

55
00:02:32,626 --> 00:02:33,526
One genus!

56
00:02:33,490 --> 00:02:38,188
A genus sits lower in the hierarchy,
so its members share more features.

57
00:02:38,188 --> 00:02:40,234
A class is bigger and looser.

58
00:02:40,884 --> 00:02:41,910
Remember this.

59
00:02:41,910 --> 00:02:44,662
Lower the taxon, more the shared features.

60
00:02:45,262 --> 00:02:47,155
One more difference to keep straight.

61
00:02:47,505 --> 00:02:51,234
For animals, the category
above class is called phylum.

62
00:02:51,234 --> 00:02:53,642
Insects belong to phylum Arthropoda.

63
00:02:54,092 --> 00:02:57,189
For plants, the same
rank is called division.

64
00:02:57,189 --> 00:02:59,703
Wheat belongs to division Angiospermae.

65
00:03:00,353 --> 00:03:02,033
Now, a worked example.

66
00:03:02,033 --> 00:03:03,255
Wheat and mango.

67
00:03:03,255 --> 00:03:04,994
Where do they part ways?

68
00:03:05,344 --> 00:03:06,799
Kingdom: both Plantae.

69
00:03:06,799 --> 00:03:07,699
Same.

70
00:03:07,749 --> 00:03:11,053
Division: both Angiospermae,
flowering plants.

71
00:03:11,053 --> 00:03:11,953
Same.

72
00:03:12,110 --> 00:03:15,997
Class: wheat is Monocotyledonae,
mango is Dicotyledonae.

73
00:03:15,997 --> 00:03:17,300
Here they split.

74
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:20,869
So from class down,
every category differs.

75
00:03:20,869 --> 00:03:24,306
Wheat's family is Poaceae,
mango's is Anacardiaceae.

76
00:03:24,956 --> 00:03:26,421
Now, the exam trap.

77
00:03:26,771 --> 00:03:30,741
Many students say members of a kingdom
share more features than members

78
00:03:30,741 --> 00:03:31,641
of a genus.

79
00:03:32,041 --> 00:03:33,505
But it is the reverse.

80
00:03:33,505 --> 00:03:37,832
The kingdom is the biggest group, so
its members share the fewest features.

81
00:03:38,482 --> 00:03:39,970
Where is this asked?

82
00:03:39,970 --> 00:03:44,582
As match the column questions: an
organism's family, order and class, or

83
00:03:44,582 --> 00:03:46,529
the order of the categories.

84
00:03:47,129 --> 00:03:48,289
Exam level now.

85
00:03:48,289 --> 00:03:51,014
Which pair is correctly matched for wheat?

86
00:03:51,014 --> 00:03:52,223
Pause and solve.

87
00:03:57,623 --> 00:03:59,067
The answer is C.

88
00:03:59,067 --> 00:04:01,239
Monocotyledonae is the class.

89
00:04:01,739 --> 00:04:03,997
Poales is the order, not the family.

90
00:04:03,997 --> 00:04:06,300
Poaceae is the family, not the order.

91
00:04:06,800 --> 00:04:09,950
And Angiospermae is the
division, not the class.

92
00:04:10,550 --> 00:04:12,011
Here is your recap.

93
00:04:12,011 --> 00:04:13,513
Screenshot this card.

94
00:04:16,713 --> 00:04:19,711
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

95
00:04:20,111 --> 00:04:21,011
One.

96
00:04:20,627 --> 00:04:24,364
Name the seven categories,
from kingdom down to species.

97
00:04:25,264 --> 00:04:26,164
Two.

98
00:04:25,770 --> 00:04:30,326
Why do members of a genus share more
features than members of a class?

99
00:04:31,226 --> 00:04:32,126
Three.

100
00:04:31,853 --> 00:04:35,085
At which category do
wheat and mango part ways?

101
00:04:36,185 --> 00:04:39,269
Next time: mango, potato,
housefly and human.

102
00:04:39,269 --> 00:04:41,932
What does each rank really mean for them?

103
00:04:41,932 --> 00:04:46,788
We find out in species, genus, family,
order, class, phylum and kingdom.
